 $u(t)$ describes a point moving around on the unit sphere $\\|u\\| = 1$. Show that the velocity vector $du/dt$ is orthogonal to the position: $u^{T}(du/dt) = 0$.\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 2.** Suppose you add a positive semidefinite rank two matrix $T$ to $S$. What interlacing inequalities will connect the eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of $S$ and $\\alpha$ of $S + T$?\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 3.** (a) Find the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1(t)$ and $\\lambda_2(t)$ of $A(t) = A(0) + t\\,vv^{T}$.\r\n(b) At $t = 0$, find the eigenvectors of $A(0)$ and verify $d\\lambda/dt = q^{T}(dA/dt)\\,q$.\r\n(c) Check that the change $A(t) − A(0)$ is positive semidefinite for $t > 0$. Then verify the interlacing law $\\lambda_1(t) \\ge \\lambda_1(0) \\ge \\lambda_2(t) \\ge \\lambda_2(0)$.\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 4.** $S$ is a symmetric matrix with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 \\ge \\lambda_2 \\ge \\cdots \\ge \\lambda_n$ and eigenvectors $q_1, q_2, \\ldots, q_n$. Which $i$ of those eigenvectors are a basis for an $i$-dimensional subspace $V$ with this property: The minimum of $x^{T}Sx/x^{T}x$ for $x$ in $V$ is $\\lambda_i$?\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 5.** Find the eigenvalues of these $3 \\times 3$, $2 \\times 2$, and $1 \\times 1$ matrices. Show that they are interlacing.\r\n\r\n```math\r\n\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & −1 & 0 \\\\ −1 & 2 & −1 \\\\ 0 & −1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad\r\n\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & −1 \\\\ −1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad\r\n\\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\end{bmatrix}\r\n```\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 6.** Suppose $D$ is the diagonal matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(1, 2, \\ldots, n)$ and $S$ is positive definite.\r\n1) Find the derivatives at $t = 0$ of the eigenvalues $\\lambda(t)$ of $D + tS$.\r\n2) For a small $t > 0$ show that the $\\lambda$'s interlace the numbers $1, 2, \\ldots, n$.\r\n3) For any $t > 0$, find bounds on $\\lambda_{\\min}(D + tS)$ and $\\lambda_{\\max}(D + tS)$.\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 7.** Suppose $D$ is again $\\operatorname{diag}(1, 2, \\ldots, n)$ and $E$ is any $n$ by $n$ matrix.\r\n1) Find the derivatives at $t = 0$ of the singular values $\\sigma(t)$ of $D + tE$.\r\n2) What do Weyl's inequalities say about $\\sigma_{\\max}(D + E)$ and $\\sigma_{\\min}(D + E)$?\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 8.** (a) Show that every $i$-dimensional subspace $V$ contains a nonzero vector $z$ that is a combination of $q_i, q_{i+1}, \\ldots, q_n$. (Those $q$'s span a space $Z$ of dimension $n-i+1$. Based on the dimensions $i$ and $n-i+1$, why does $Z$ intersect $V$?)\r\n(b) Why does that vector $z$ have $z^{T}Sz/z^{T}z \\le \\lambda_i$? Then explain\r\n\r\n```math\r\n\\lambda_i = \\max_{\\dim V = i}\\ \\min_{z \\in V} \\frac{z^{T}Sz}{z^{T}z}\r\n```\r\n\r\n@@PAGE:180@@（书页 177）\r\n−-- OCR 原文 −--\r\n田 2 ， lnterlacing Eigenvalues and Low Rank Signals\r\n177\r\nThe Law of lnertia\r\nDefinition If S is symmetric and C is invertible, then the matrix CTSC is\r\ncongruent t0 S ” ． Thi s is not simllanty B-ISB ！ Eigenvalues of CTSC c an change\r\nfrom eigenvalues of S, but 0 can change 豆 g That is called the Law of lne rti a ”\r\nCTSC has the same number 0f (positive) (negative) (zero) eigenvalues as S.\r\nMy favonte proof starts with C = QR (b y Gram- S chmidt) · As 娓 changes gr adually to 厶\r\ncTSC changes gradually to QTSQ = Q-ISQ. N ow we do have similarity (Q-ISQ\r\nhas the same eigenvalues as S). If is invertible all the way to 厶 then no eigenvalues\r\ncan cro s s zero on the way. r g are the same for CTSC and Q-ISQ and S.\r\nThe max-mn principles also prove the Law of lnertia.\r\nIf S = LDLT has nonzero pivots in elimination, show that the signs 0f the pivots\r\n9\r\nof S ()n D) match the signs 0f the eigenvalues of & Ap p ly the Law to S and D.\r\n10 Show that this 2 × 2 KKT m atrix H has positive d negative eigenvalues ：\r\nS positive definite\r\nC invertible\r\nS C\r\nThe first pivots 什 om S are positive. The last pivots come fro m —CTs¯1c.\r\n11 The KKT matrix H is symmetnc and indefinite—this problem c ounts eigenvalues ·\r\nS ÄT\r\nas in equation （ 17 ）\r\n0 m\r\nH comes 什 Om 皿 ni 皿 zin g (positive definite) with m constraints AC = b.\r\nElinunation on H begins with S. We know its pivots are all positive.\r\nThen elimin ation multiplies S 一 1 time s [ S AT ] an d subtracts 什 om [ 0 ]\r\nto g et [ 0 一 S 一 1 T ] · That S chur complement 一 S 一 1 T is negative definite ·\r\nWhy ？ Th en the last m pivots of H (coming 什 om 一 S 一 1 T ） are negative.\r\n12 If cT sc > 0 for all 0 an d C is invertible ， why is (CY)TS(CY) also positive ？\r\nThis shows ag ain that if S has all positive eigenvalues, so does CTsc.\r\n\r\n−-- 现有译文（含译者注） −--\r\n<!-- page 180 −->\r\n\r\n> <span style=color:#7f8c8d;>本页为书页 177，前半为 §III.2 正文「The Law of Inertia（惯性定律）」的定义与证明思路，后半为 Problem Set III.2 的第 9–12 题（保留英文原题）；页眉与页码为重复元素，不译。</span>\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[section]**</span>\r\n\r\n### The Law of Inertia（惯性定律）\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[definition]**</span>\r\n\r\n**Definition（定义）** 若 $S$ 对称（symmetric）而 $C$ 可逆（invertible），则矩阵 $C^{T}SC$ 与 $S$ congruent（合同）。这**不是** similarity（相似变换）$B^{-1}SB$！$C^{T}SC$ 的特征值可以与 $S$ 的特征值不同，但它们相对于零的位置、也就是正负号不会改变。这正是所谓 Law of Inertia（惯性定律）：$C^{T}SC$ 拥有与 $S$ 相同数目的正特征值、负特征值与零特征值。\r\n\r\n> <span style=color:#1e8449;>**[note] 译者注（第一性原理）：**</span> 这里最容易混的一对概念是 congruence（合同）与 similarity（相似）：$C^TSC$ 与 $B^{-1}SB$。区别不在记号而在**什么被保持不变**：相似变换保持特征值本身，合同变换只保持特征值的正负号个数。为什么？把三个事实按顺序串起来就明白了。补一层机制：相似变换 $B^{-1}SB$ 说的是「同一个线性变换换一组基之后的矩阵」，特征值是线性变换本身的内禀量，换基当然读出来一模一样；合同变换 $C^TSC$ 说的是「同一个二次型换一组坐标之后的矩阵」，$z^TSz$ 的数值会被坐标缩放改变，但「这是碗还是马鞍」这种定性信息改不了。第一，$C=QR$ 是 Gram–Schmidt 的副产品，$Q$ 正交、$R$ 可逆；于是 $C^TSC=R^TQ^TSQR=R^T(Q^TSQ)R$，而 $Q^TSQ=Q^{-1}SQ$ 已经是相似变换，特征值一个不差。第二，同理可用 $R^{-1}$ 把 $R^T(Q^TSQ)R$ 变成 $(R^{-1})^T(Q^TSQ)R^{-1}$ 的合同形式，继续往里塞逆矩阵，特征值会被乘上正的缩放因子 $r^2$，但**正负号不会被翻转**。第三，也是关键的一步：从 $C$ 连续变形到 $Q$ 时，中间每个矩阵都保持可逆，而实对称矩阵的特征值是矩阵元素的连续函数；若某个特征值要从正变负，它必须连续地经过 $0$，即某个中间矩阵奇异——与可逆性矛盾。于是途中没有特征值穿越零点，正、负、零的个数被「锁死」。代一组数字验算：取 $S=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$，特征值 $\\lambda=2\\pm1$，即 $3$ 与 $1$，两个都为正；取 $C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$，$\\det C=1$ 故可逆。则 $C^TSC=\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\3&6\\end{pmatrix}$，特征方程 $\\lambda^2-8\\lambda+3=0$，得 $\\lambda=4\\pm\\sqrt{13}\\approx7.606$ 与 $0.394$：两个特征值全被拉大变样，符号却原封不动，正好对上「相似保数值、合同只保符号」。这条结论有直接的工程用途：对 $S$ 做消元得到 $S=LDL^T$，$L$ 可逆所以 $S$ 与对角阵 $D$ 合同，因此 $D$ 的对角元（主元）的正负号个数就精确等于特征值的正负号个数。**这让你不必求特征值、只做一遍消元就能数出正负惯性指数**，也正是习题 10、11 里判断 KKT 矩阵正负特征值个数的依据——那里 $S$ 正定、末尾的 Schur 补 $-AS^{-1}A^T$ 负定，主元符号直接读出来。把它整理成**惯性三问**这条模板：一问变换类型——是 $B^{-1}SB$（相似，特征值原样）还是 $C^TSC$（合同，仅符号）？二问所求的量在不在不变量清单里——秩、惯性指数、对称性在；特征值、行列式、迹只在 $C$ 正交时保留。三问能不能用消元把答案读出来——写出 $S=LDL^T$，直接数 $D$ 的对角元符号。跨课程连接：这里「数维数」的直觉与 6.042J 的秩-零化度同源，但惯性指数数的不是「有几个维度」而是「特征值落在正半轴、负半轴、零点上的个数」，它比维数更粗，所以合同这种比相似更松的关系刚好够用；CSAPP 浮点一节的分析则解释了这条判据为何值得记住：求特征值要走迭代的 QR 算法，代价与舍入误差都更高，而做一遍消元只需约 $n^3/3$ 次乘加且向后稳定，主元符号在浮点下也不容易错。名字里的「惯性」取的正是几何里的意思：二次型 $z^TSz=1$ 的等高线只有在符号组合改变时才会从椭圆变成双曲线，单纯缩放只是把同一张图拉伸。还有一个常见误判要堵住：既然符号被保住，是不是只看行列式的正负就够了？不够，$n\\ge3$ 时行列式的符号只反映零特征值个数的奇偶性，例如两个负特征值配一个正特征值同样让行列式为正，而惯性指数把正、负、零三类个数分别报出来，信息严格更多。\r\n\r\n\r\n我最喜欢的证明从 $C = QR$ 出发，这一步由 Gram-Schmidt（格拉姆-施密特）给出。当 $C$ 逐渐变到 $Q$ 时，$C^{T}SC$ 也逐渐变到 $Q^{T}SQ = Q^{-1}SQ$，此时我们确实拥有了 similarity（相似性）——$Q^{-1}SQ$ 与 $S$ 有相同的特征值。如果从 $C$ 一路到 $Q$ 的每个中间矩阵都可逆，那么途中不可能有特征值穿过零。于是正的、负的、零的特征值个数，对 $C^{T}SC$、$Q^{-1}SQ$ 与 $S$ 三者完全相同。\r\n\r\nmax-min principles（极大极小原理）同样能证明 Law of Inertia（惯性定律）。\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 9.** If $S = LDL^{T}$ has nonzero pivots in elimination, show that the signs of the pivots of $S$ (on $D$) match the signs of the eigenvalues of $S$. Apply the Law [of Inertia] to $S$ and $D$.\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 10.** Show that this $2 \\times 2$ KKT matrix $H$ has positive and negative eigenvalues:\r\n\r\n```math\r\nH = \\begin{bmatrix} S & C^{T} \\\\ C & 0 \\end{bmatrix}\r\n\\qquad S \\text{ positive definite},\\ C \\text{ invertible}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first pivots from $S$ are positive. The last pivots come from $-CS^{-1}C^{T}$.\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 11.** The KKT matrix $H$ is symmetric and indefinite—this problem counts eigenvalues as in equation (17):\r\n\r\n```math\r\nH = \\begin{bmatrix} S & A^{T} \\\\ A & 0 \\end{bmatrix}\r\n\\qquad (n+m) \\text{ by } (n+m)\r\n```\r\n\r\n$H$ comes from minimizing $\\frac{1}{2}x^{T}Sx − x^{T}b$ (positive definite) with $m$ constraints $Ax = b$. Elimination on $H$ begins with $S$. We know its pivots are all positive. Then elimination multiplies $S^{-1}$ times $[\\,S\\ \\ A^{T}\\,]$ and subtracts from $[\\,A\\ \\ 0\\,]$ to get $[\\,0\\ \\ −AS^{-1}A^{T}\\,]$. That Schur complement $-AS^{-1}A^{T}$ is negative definite. Why? Then the last $m$ pivots of $H$ (coming from $-AS^{-1}A^{T}$) are negative.\r\n\r\n<span style=color:#2471a3;>**[problem]**</span> **Problem 12.** If $x^{T}Sx > 0$ for all $x$ and $C$ is invertible, why is $(Cy)^{T}S(Cy)$ also positive? This shows again that if $S$ has all positive eigenvalues, so does $C^{T}SC$."
